<div id="connection" role="status" hidden></div>
<main id="deck">
<section class="slide active" id="s0" aria-label="Title">
 <div class="kicker">FINA 402 · Investments · Lecture 07</div><h1>What Is a<br>Share Worth?</h1><div class="subtitle">Discounted cash flows and dividend discount models: from expected dividends to a price, and a trading signal.</div>
 <div class="title-questions"><div><span>01</span><h3>How do we turn future<br>dividends into a price today?</h3></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Is the market price<br>too high or too low?</h3></div></div>
 <div class="meta">Valuation intuition → DCF framework → one-stage DDM → two-stage DDM &amp; terminal value.<br>Two individual activities · Gordon model &amp; trading signal · Two-stage valuation · Readings: Ch. 12.7, 13.2, 13.3</div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s1"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Opening case · a real valuation</div><h2>What was Discover worth?</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>February 2024: Capital One agrees to buy Discover</h3><p class="small">Offer: 1.0192 Capital One shares per Discover share, worth <b>$139.86</b>, versus a Discover price of <b>$110.49</b> the day before the announcement: a 26.6% premium. Was that a fair price? Each side hired an investment bank to value Discover, and both used a <b>dividend discount model</b>.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><table class="w-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>PJT Partners (Discover's advisor)</th><th>Centerview (Capital One's advisor)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cash flows</td><td>Dividendable capital 2024–27</td><td>Dividendable capital 2024–30</td></tr><tr><td>Terminal value</td><td>8.5x–10.5x 2028E net income</td><td>8.5x–10.5x 2031E net income</td></tr><tr><td>Discount rate k</td><td>11.0%–13.0% (CAPM)</td><td>12.75%–13.75% (CAPM)</td></tr><tr><td>Value per share</td><td><b>$117.46–$145.00</b></td><td><b>$120.50–$141.75</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p class="tiny muted">“Dividendable” cash flow = earnings the bank can pay out (dividends and buybacks) while keeping its regulatory capital ratio (CET1) at target. Source: Capital One Form S-4/A, SEC, Dec. 2024 (fairness opinions and prospective financial information).</p></div>
  <div class="prompt">Was $139.86 a fair price for Discover's shareholders?</div><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-reveal="dfs-answer">Discuss, then reveal</button><div id="dfs-answer" class="reveal note-box" hidden>The offer sits inside both banks' DDM ranges, above the market price and near the top of what Discover's own dividends justify. By the end of today you will be able to build this kind of valuation yourself, and see why most of the value comes from the terminal value.</div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Football field · value per Discover share <span>replicate PJT with the public numbers</span></div><canvas id="dfs-chart" aria-label="Football field chart: market price, offer, PJT and Centerview ranges, and the replication"></canvas>
  <div class="two-inputs" style="margin-top:8px"><div><label for="dfs-k">Cost of equity k <strong id="dfs-k-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="dfs-k" min="1000" max="1400" value="1200" step="25"></div><div><label for="dfs-m">Exit P/E multiple <strong id="dfs-m-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="dfs-m" min="70" max="120" value="95" step="5"></div></div>
  <div id="dfs-stats" class="stat-row" style="margin-top:8px"></div><p class="tiny muted">Replication: PV at end-2023 of dividendable capital $2.0, $2.6, $2.6, $2.8 bn (2024–27) plus TV = multiple × 2028E net income of $4.0 bn, ÷ 252.5 m diluted shares. Rounded public inputs and simple year-end discounting put it a few percent below PJT's own figures.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s2"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part I · General intuition</div><h2>Every asset is worth the present value of its cash flows</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="math-card"><div class="math-label">The general approach, for any financial instrument</div><div class="formula large-formula">P = CF<sub>1</sub>/(1+r) + CF<sub>2</sub>/(1+r)<sup>2</sup> + … + CF<sub>n</sub>/(1+r)<sup>n</sup></div><div class="math-line">Bonds (later in the course): cash flows are promised and known.</div><div class="math-line">Equity: cash flows are <b>uncertain</b>, so we must ask:</div></div>
  <div class="card"><table class="w-table"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>In the formula</th><th>How we answer it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What cash flows?</td><td>CF<sub>t</sub></td><td>Dividends or free cash flows</td></tr><tr><td>What discount rate?</td><td>r</td><td>Cost of equity from the CAPM (Lecture 06)</td></tr><tr><td>What horizon?</td><td>n</td><td>Life cycle of the firm: one or two stages</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Cash flows vs. their present values <span>CF<sub>1</sub> = $2.00, growing 3% a year</span></div><canvas id="pv-chart" aria-label="Bars of each year's cash flow and its present value"></canvas>
  <div class="two-inputs" style="margin-top:8px"><div><label for="pv-r">Discount rate r <strong id="pv-r-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="pv-r" min="40" max="150" value="80" step="5"></div><div><label for="pv-n">Horizon n <strong id="pv-n-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="pv-n" min="5" max="60" value="30" step="1"></div></div>
  <div id="pv-stats" class="stat-row" style="margin-top:8px"></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s3"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part I · DCF framework</div><h2>Three components of a DCF model</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>Two proxies for cash flows</h3><table class="w-table"><thead><tr><th>Cash flow</th><th>Model</th><th>Values</th><th>Discount rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Dividends</b></td><td>Dividend discount model (DDM)</td><td>Equity</td><td>Cost of equity k (CAPM)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Free cash flows</b></td><td>Free cash flow model</td><td>The whole firm</td><td>Cost of capital (WACC)</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="small">Both the timing and the size of future cash flows are uncertain, and in principle there are infinitely many to forecast. The <b>life cycle</b> of the firm is how we reduce that number.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt">Why do we discount dividends at the cost of equity and not at the WACC?</div><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-reveal="fw-answer">Discuss, then reveal</button><div id="fw-answer" class="reveal note-box" hidden>Dividends go only to shareholders, so they are discounted at the return shareholders require (k). Free cash flows to the firm go to all capital providers, debt and equity, so they are discounted at the weighted average cost of capital.</div></div>
 <div class="col-r"><div class="card"><div class="step-number">01</div><h3>The life cycle of the asset</h3><p>Mature firms → <b>one-stage</b> models. Firms still growing fast → <b>multi-stage</b> models.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="step-number">02</div><h3>Future cash flows</h3><p>The key component of any DCF, and the hardest one to estimate.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="step-number">03</div><h3>Discount rates</h3><p>Cost of equity, often from the CAPM: k = r<sub>f</sub> + β(E[r<sub>M</sub>] − r<sub>f</sub>), for equity valuation. WACC for firm valuation.</p></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s4"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part I · How many stages?</div><h2>The life cycle of an industry</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l card"><div class="chart-heading">Earnings and earnings growth over the life cycle <span>click a stage</span></div><canvas id="life-chart" aria-label="Earnings follow an S-curve across stages A to D"></canvas>
  <div class="quick-weights"><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-stage="A">A · Start-up</button><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-stage="B">B · Consolidation</button><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-stage="C">C · Maturity</button><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-stage="D">D · Decline</button></div></div>
 <div class="col-r"><div class="card" id="stage-info"></div>
  <div class="note-box">Where the firm sits in its life cycle decides the model: a firm already in stage C can be valued with one constant growth rate; a firm in stage A or B needs a high-growth stage first, then a stable one.</div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s5"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Contrast case · a young company</div><h2>SpaceX: no dividends, negative cash flows</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>Why a DDM cannot value SpaceX</h3><p class="small">SpaceX went public on June 12, 2026 at <b>$135</b> a share (about $1.8 trillion) and closed its first day at <b>$161</b>. In 2025 it had revenues of $18.7 bn, an operating loss, and pays <b>no dividends</b>: all three businesses (launch, Starlink, xAI) are in stages A–B of the life cycle. So we value the whole firm with <b>free cash flows to the firm (FCFF)</b>, discounted at the WACC, in two stages.</p></div>
  <div class="card"><table class="w-table"><thead><tr><th colspan="2">Aswath Damodaran's DCF (NYU Stern), June 2026</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>High growth, years 1–10</td><td>Revenues $18.7 bn → $420 bn · operating margin 8% → 38%</td></tr><tr><td>Free cash flow</td><td>−$7.2 bn (yr 1), −$5.8 bn (yr 2) … +$105.5 bn (yr 10)</td></tr><tr><td>Stable growth, after year 10</td><td>g = 4.56% · WACC 8.37% → 8.25%</td></tr><tr><td>Terminal value</td><td>$2.37 trillion → PV $1.06 trillion</td></tr><tr><td>Value of equity</td><td><b>$1.30 trillion ≈ $98 per share</b></td></tr></tbody></table><p class="tiny muted">Source: A. Damodaran, “Revisiting the SpaceX Valuation: A Post-Prospectus Update!”, Musings on Markets, Jun. 4, 2026 (inputs from the SpaceX prospectus). Prices: CNBC (IPO, first day); about $150 in early October 2026.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt">The stock trades near $150. What long-run operating margin is the market assuming?</div><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-reveal="spx-answer">Use the slider, then reveal</button><div id="spx-answer" class="reveal note-box" hidden>Keeping Damodaran's other inputs, about <b>58%</b>, versus his 38%. The price is a bet on much higher long-run profitability (or faster growth). Small changes in the terminal assumptions move the value by tens of dollars per share, because about 87% of the value is terminal.</div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Free cash flow to the firm and value per share <span>$ billions · drag the sliders</span></div><canvas id="spx-chart" aria-label="SpaceX free cash flows by year and value per share compared with market prices"></canvas>
  <div class="two-inputs" style="margin-top:8px"><div><label for="spx-m">Long-run operating margin <strong id="spx-m-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="spx-m" min="200" max="650" value="381" step="1"></div><div><label for="spx-k">Change in WACC <strong id="spx-k-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="spx-k" min="-100" max="200" value="0" step="25"></div><div><label for="spx-g">Stable growth g <strong id="spx-g-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="spx-g" min="200" max="500" value="456" step="2"></div><div></div></div>
  <div id="spx-stats" class="stat-row" style="margin-top:8px"></div><p class="tiny muted">Replicates Damodaran within 0.1%; reinvestment held fixed.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s6"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part II · Dividend discount models</div><h2>The one-stage DDM: the Gordon model</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>Cash flows to equity are dividends</h3><p>If δ is the dividend payout ratio, dividends per share follow from earnings per share: <b>DPS = EPS × δ</b>. Earnings forecasts → future dividends.</p></div>
  <div class="math-card"><div class="math-label">Assumptions · one discount rate k, dividends grow at a constant rate g</div><div class="math-line">D<sub>1</sub> = D<sub>0</sub>(1+g), D<sub>2</sub> = D<sub>1</sub>(1+g), D<sub>3</sub> = D<sub>2</sub>(1+g), …</div><div class="math-line">P<sub>0</sub> = D<sub>1</sub>/(1+k) + D<sub>1</sub>(1+g)/(1+k)<sup>2</sup> + D<sub>1</sub>(1+g)<sup>2</sup>/(1+k)<sup>3</sup> + …</div><div class="formula result">P<sub>0</sub> = D<sub>1</sub> / (k − g) = D<sub>0</sub>(1 + g) / (k − g)</div><div class="math-line">The whole stream is pinned down by two inputs: D<sub>0</sub> and g (given k).</div></div></div>
 <div class="col-r"><div class="card"><h3>Applicable to mature firms</h3><p>Also called the <b>constant</b> or <b>stable growth</b> model. It fits mature firms, or the stable-growth stage of a non-mature firm: beta, leverage and dividends are reasonably stable over time.</p><p class="small">Notation: D<sub>0</sub> is the dividend just paid; D<sub>1</sub> is next year's expected dividend. Use D<sub>1</sub> in the numerator.</p></div>
  <div class="prompt">What happens to the formula if g gets close to k, or exceeds it?</div><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-reveal="gk-answer">Discuss, then reveal</button><div id="gk-answer" class="reveal note-box" hidden>As g approaches k the price explodes, and for g ≥ k the formula gives a meaningless (infinite or negative) value. No firm can grow faster than its discount rate forever, so the stable growth rate must stay well below k, typically near the long-run growth of the economy.</div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s7"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part II · Estimating the inputs</div><h2>Growth from fundamentals: g = b × ROE</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="math-card"><div class="math-label">Fundamental growth in earnings</div><div class="formula">g = b × ROE</div><div class="math-line">b = retention ratio = 1 − δ = reinvested earnings / earnings</div><div class="math-line">ROE = earnings / book value of equity</div><div class="math-line">Growth depends on <b>how much</b> the firm reinvests (b) and <b>what return</b> those projects earn (ROE).</div></div>
  <div class="card"><label for="gm-eps">EPS<sub>0</sub> <strong id="gm-eps-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="gm-eps" min="100" max="1000" value="500" step="10"><label for="gm-d">Payout ratio δ <strong id="gm-d-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="gm-d" min="20" max="100" value="60" step="1"><label for="gm-roe">ROE <strong id="gm-roe-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="gm-roe" min="40" max="200" value="100" step="5"><label for="gm-k">Cost of equity k <strong id="gm-k-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="gm-k" min="50" max="150" value="80" step="5"></div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Gordon price vs. payout ratio <span>D<sub>0</sub> = EPS<sub>0</sub> × δ · g = (1 − δ) × ROE</span></div><canvas id="gordon-chart" aria-label="Gordon model price as a function of the payout ratio"></canvas><div id="gordon-stats" class="stat-row"></div><div id="gordon-note" class="note-box" style="margin-top:8px"></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s8"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part II · Worked example</div><h2>Example 1: equity value and a trading signal</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>Given market data</h3><table class="w-table"><tbody><tr><td>Dividend just paid</td><td>D<sub>0</sub> = $2.86</td></tr><tr><td>Expected dividend growth</td><td>g = 3.07%</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of equity</td><td>k = 6.46%</td></tr><tr><td>Market price</td><td>$75.77</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="small">Is there a trading signal?</p><button class="btn btn-gold" data-reveal="ex1-answer">Reveal the solution</button><div id="ex1-answer" class="reveal" hidden><div class="math-line">D<sub>1</sub> = 2.86 × 1.0307 = $2.948</div><div class="math-line result">P<sub>0</sub> = 2.948 / (0.0646 − 0.0307) = $86.96</div><div class="math-line">P<sub>model</sub> $86.96 &gt; P<sub>market</sub> $75.77 → <b>undervalued → buy (go long)</b></div></div></div>
  <div class="prompt">What cost of equity is the market price implying?</div><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-reveal="ex1-implied">Reveal</button><div id="ex1-implied" class="reveal note-box" hidden>Solve 75.77 = 2.948 / (k − 0.0307): k = 2.948/75.77 + 3.07% = <b>6.96%</b>. The market discounts this stock at about 0.5 pp more than our 6.46%. The signal is only as good as our estimate of k.</div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Model price vs. cost of equity <span>D<sub>1</sub> = $2.948 · g = 3.07%</span></div><canvas id="ex1-chart" aria-label="Gordon price falls as the cost of equity rises; market price line at 75.77"></canvas><label for="ex1-k" style="margin-top:8px">Cost of equity k <strong id="ex1-k-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="ex1-k" min="450" max="1000" value="646" step="1"><div id="ex1-stats" class="stat-row"></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide activity-slide" id="s9" data-activity="1"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Activity 1 · Individual submission · 8 min · 3 pts</div><h2>Your company: Gordon value and trading signal</h2><p>Estimate growth from fundamentals and the cost of equity from the CAPM, value the stock with the Gordon model, and compare with its market price.</p></div><div class="activity-host" id="activity-1"></div></section>
<section class="slide" id="s10"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part III · Multi-stage models</div><h2>The two-stage DDM</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>Main assumptions on the life of the firm</h3><p>An initial <b>high-growth (HG)</b> period that lasts n years (say, 4 or 5), followed by a <b>stable-growth (SG)</b> stage from year n+1 to infinity.</p>
   <div style="position:relative;margin:26px 6px 10px;height:64px" aria-label="Timeline: high-growth stage from year 0 to year n, stable-growth stage from year n to infinity"><div style="position:absolute;left:0;right:0;top:22px;height:3px;background:var(--border)"></div><div style="position:absolute;left:0;width:45%;top:20px;height:7px;background:var(--gold);border-radius:4px"></div><div style="position:absolute;left:45%;right:0;top:20px;height:7px;background:var(--accent);opacity:.75;border-radius:4px"></div>
    <div style="position:absolute;left:0;top:-6px;font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gold)">HG stage · g<sub>H</sub>, k<sub>H</sub></div><div style="position:absolute;left:47%;top:-6px;font-size:.72rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--accent)">SG stage · g<sub>S</sub>, k<sub>S</sub></div>
    <div style="position:absolute;left:0;top:34px;font-size:.72rem">Year 0</div><div style="position:absolute;left:45%;transform:translateX(-50%);top:34px;font-size:.72rem">Year n</div><div style="position:absolute;right:0;top:30px;font-size:1rem">∞</div></div>
   <p class="small"><b>Notation:</b> year n means the end of year n and the beginning of year n+1. If a growth model is used in the HG stage, g<sub>H</sub> &gt; g<sub>S</sub>. The discount rates k<sub>H</sub> and k<sub>S</sub> can also differ.</p></div></div>
 <div class="col-r"><div class="card"><h3>HG and SG growth rates</h3><p class="small">Dividends in the HG stage do not need to grow at a constant rate; then g<sub>H</sub> is their average growth rate. In the SG stage dividends grow at a constant g<sub>S</sub>, which should not be too high.</p><p class="small">Example: D<sub>1</sub> = $1.00, D<sub>2</sub> = $1.13, D<sub>3</sub> = $1.26, D<sub>4</sub> = $1.40. What is the average growth rate?</p><button class="btn btn-gold" data-reveal="gh-answer">Reveal</button><div id="gh-answer" class="reveal" hidden><div class="math-line">Year-by-year: 13.00%, 11.50%, 11.11%</div><div class="math-line result">g<sub>H</sub> = (1.40 / 1.00)<sup>1/3</sup> − 1 = 11.87%</div><p class="tiny muted">Three growth periods between four dividends. The arithmetic average of the three rates (11.87%) is almost identical here.</p></div></div>
  <div class="note-box">Implement it in two steps: (1) value the SG stage at year n, the <b>terminal value</b>; (2) discount the HG dividends and the terminal value back to year 0. This is exactly PJT's Discover model: four years of dividends, then a terminal value at the end of 2027.</div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s11"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part III · Terminal value</div><h2>Two-stage formulas: most of the value is terminal</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="math-card"><div class="math-label">Step 1 · terminal value at year n (Gordon model on the SG stage)</div><div class="formula">TV<sub>n</sub> = D<sub>n+1</sub> / (k<sub>S</sub> − g<sub>S</sub>) = D<sub>n</sub>(1 + g<sub>S</sub>) / (k<sub>S</sub> − g<sub>S</sub>)</div><div class="math-label" style="margin-top:10px">Step 2 · value today</div><div class="formula result">P<sub>0</sub> = Σ<sub>t=1..n</sub> D<sub>t</sub>/(1+k<sub>H</sub>)<sup>t</sup> + TV<sub>n</sub>/(1+k<sub>H</sub>)<sup>n</sup></div><div class="math-line">TV<sub>n</sub> is the value at year n of all dividends D<sub>n+1</sub>, D<sub>n+2</sub>, … · g<sub>S</sub> is often set near industry or GDP growth.</div></div>
  <div class="card"><div class="two-inputs"><div><label for="ts-d0">D<sub>0</sub> <strong id="ts-d0-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="ts-d0" min="50" max="300" value="100" step="5"></div><div><label for="ts-n">HG years n <strong id="ts-n-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="ts-n" min="1" max="10" value="4" step="1"></div><div><label for="ts-gh">High growth g<sub>H</sub> <strong id="ts-gh-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="ts-gh" min="0" max="300" value="120" step="5"></div><div><label for="ts-gs">Stable growth g<sub>S</sub> <strong id="ts-gs-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="ts-gs" min="0" max="80" value="40" step="5"></div><div><label for="ts-k">Discount rate k <strong id="ts-k-label"></strong></label><input type="range" id="ts-k" min="60" max="160" value="100" step="5"></div><div></div></div></div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Where today's value comes from <span>present values, one discount rate k<sub>H</sub> = k<sub>S</sub> = k</span></div><canvas id="ts-chart" aria-label="Present value of each high-growth dividend and of the terminal value"></canvas><div id="ts-stats" class="stat-row"></div></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide" id="s12"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Part III · Worked example</div><h2>Example 2: a two-stage valuation</h2></div>
 <div class="slide-body"><div class="col-l"><div class="card"><h3>Assumptions</h3><p class="small">High-growth stage: 4 years, 2022–2025 (today = end of 2021). One discount rate, k<sub>H</sub> = k<sub>S</sub> = k. Market data on the company:</p><table class="w-table"><tbody><tr><td>Dividends</td><td>D<sub>2022</sub> = $1.00 · D<sub>2023</sub> = $1.13 · D<sub>2024</sub> = $1.26 · D<sub>2025</sub> = $1.40</td></tr><tr><td>Beta · CAPM</td><td>β = 1.20 · r<sub>f</sub> = 4.3% · market risk premium 6.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Stable stage</td><td>ROE = 16% · payout δ = 0.52</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <div class="math-card"><div class="math-line">k = 4.3% + 1.20 × 6.0% = <b>11.50%</b></div><div class="math-line">g<sub>S</sub> = b × ROE = (1 − 0.52) × 16% = <b>7.68%</b></div><button class="btn btn-gold" data-reveal="ex2-answer">Reveal the valuation</button><div id="ex2-answer" class="reveal" hidden><div class="math-line">D<sub>2026</sub> = 1.40 × 1.0768 = $1.5075 → TV<sub>2025</sub> = 1.5075 / (0.1150 − 0.0768) = <b>$39.46</b></div><div class="math-line">PV of dividends = 1.00/1.115 + 1.13/1.115² + 1.26/1.115³ + 1.40/1.115⁴ = 0.897 + 0.909 + 0.909 + 0.906 = $3.62</div><div class="math-line">PV of TV = 39.46 / 1.115⁴ = $25.53</div><div class="math-line result">P<sub>0</sub> = 3.62 + 25.53 = $29.15</div></div></div></div>
 <div class="col-r card"><div class="chart-heading">Building the price <span>present values at k = 11.5%</span></div><div id="ex2-wait" class="compact-note muted" style="padding:30px 10px;text-align:center">Reveal the valuation to draw the chart.</div><canvas id="ex2-chart" aria-label="Present values of the four dividends and the terminal value" hidden></canvas><div id="ex2-stats" class="stat-row"></div><p class="tiny muted">A stable growth rate of 7.68% is high relative to long-run GDP growth; try a lower g<sub>S</sub> on the previous slide to see how sensitive the price is.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class="slide activity-slide" id="s13" data-activity="2"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">Activity 2 · Individual submission · 8 min · 2 pts</div><h2>Your company: a two-stage valuation</h2><p>Compute the terminal value at year 3 and the value of the share today.</p></div><div class="activity-host" id="activity-2"></div></section>
<section class="slide" id="s14"><div class="slide-header"><div class="eyebrow">The story, in three ideas</div><h2>What is a share worth?</h2></div>
 <div class="three-cards takeaway"><div class="card"><div class="step-number">01</div><h3>Value = PV of expected cash flows</h3><p>For equity the cash flows are dividends, discounted at the cost of equity k from the CAPM. The life cycle tells us how many stages to model.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="step-number">02</div><h3>Gordon model for mature firms</h3><p>P<sub>0</sub> = D<sub>1</sub>/(k − g), with g = b × ROE. Very sensitive to k − g. Compare with the market price: model above market → buy; below → sell.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="step-number">03</div><h3>Two stages: the TV dominates</h3><p>Discount the high-growth dividends and TV<sub>n</sub> = D<sub>n+1</sub>/(k − g<sub>S</sub>). Most of the value usually comes from the terminal value.</p></div></div>
 <div class="closing-question">Back to Discover: in PJT's valuation, what share of the value comes from the terminal value?</div><button class="btn btn-ghost" data-reveal="closing-answer">Reveal</button><div id="closing-answer" class="reveal note-box" hidden>About three quarters. With k = 12% and a 9.5x multiple, the PV of the terminal value is $24.15 bn of $31.64 bn (76%), or $125.30 per share. In Example 2 it was 88%. Real or textbook, the valuation rests mostly on the terminal assumptions, so that is where analysts (and the two banks) disagree.</div><p class="small muted">Before leaving: confirm both activities show “Saved to class”. Preview or demonstration results do not count as submissions.</p>
</section>
</main>
